% ── Abstract ──────────────────────────────────────────
\begin{abstract}
We propose a reinforcement learning framework based on Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) to fine-tune Vision-Language Models (VLMs) for industrial anomaly detection (IAD).
Starting from a Supervised Fine-Tuned (SFT) Qwen2.5-VL-7B model, we design a composite reward function combining answer accuracy, defect type classification, and bounding box localization.
Our approach achieves a +5.26pp improvement in Recall@IoU=0.8 (38.10\% $\rightarrow$ 43.36\%), while maintaining stable performance at IoU=0.5.
Through detailed analysis, we show that RL primarily enhances the model's ability to produce high-quality localizations for borderline samples, rather than acquiring new detection capabilities.
We further investigate reward design, data filtering, and resolution effects, providing insights for future work on VLM-based anomaly detection.
\end{abstract}

% ── 1. Introduction ───────────────────────────────────
\section{Introduction}

Industrial anomaly detection is critical for manufacturing quality control, where identifying defects such as scratches, pitting, and corrosion directly impacts product safety and reliability.
Traditional approaches rely on reconstruction-based methods (e.g., autoencoders) or embedding-based anomaly scoring, but these often lack the ability to provide interpretable defect descriptions.

Recent works have explored Vision-Language Models (VLMs) for anomaly detection due to their ability to generate both detection results and natural language descriptions.
However, supervised fine-tuning (SFT) alone has limitations in localization accuracy, particularly for small or ambiguous defects near detection boundaries.

Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) and its variants have shown remarkable success in improving language model alignment.
Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO)~\citep{shao2024deepseekmath} offers an efficient alternative that eliminates the critic model by computing advantages relative to a sampled group.

In this paper, we apply GRPO to VLM-based industrial anomaly detection.
We design a composite reward function that combines three components: answer accuracy ($R_{ans}$), defect type classification ($R_{cls}$), and bounding box localization ($R_{loc}$) using CIoU.

Our key contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item A GRPO training framework for VLM-based industrial anomaly detection
    \item A composite reward function with CIoU-based location reward
    \item Detailed analysis showing RL improves localization precision for borderline samples (IoU $\in$ [0.7, 0.8)) rather than learning new detection capabilities
    \item Ablation studies on reward design, data filtering, and input resolution
\end{enumerate}

% ── 2. Related Work ───────────────────────────────────
\section{Related Work}

\subsection{Industrial Anomaly Detection}
Traditional industrial anomaly detection methods can be categorized into reconstruction-based and embedding-based approaches.
Reconstruction methods~\citep{bergmann2019mvtec} learn to reconstruct normal patterns and detect anomalies as reconstruction residuals.
Embedding-based methods~\citep{roth2022towards} learn feature representations where normal and anomalous samples are separable.
Recent works have explored VLMs~\citep{li2023industrial} for their ability to provide both detection and natural language descriptions.

\subsection{Reinforcement Learning for VLMs}
RLHF~\citep{ouyang2022training} has been successful in aligning language models with human preferences.
DPO~\citep{rafailov2023direct} simplifies the pipeline by eliminating the reward model.
GRPO~\citep{shao2024deepseekmath} further improves efficiency by computing group-relative advantages without a critic.
These approaches have been primarily applied to language tasks, with limited exploration in vision-based applications.

% ── 3. Method ─────────────────────────────────────────
\section{Method}

\subsection{Problem Formulation}
Given an industrial image $x$, the VLM generates a text response $y$ containing defect descriptions and bounding boxes:
\begin{equation}
    y = \text{``Yes, a '\{type\}' defect was found at \{bbox\_2d: $[x_1, y_1, x_2, y_2]$\}.'' }
\end{equation}

\subsection{GRPO Training}
GRPO samples $K$ responses $\{y_1, ..., y_K\}$ per prompt and computes rewards $\{r_1, ..., r_K\}$.
The advantage for each sample is:
\begin{equation}
    A_i = \frac{r_i - \text{mean}(\{r_1, ..., r_K\})}{\text{std}(\{r_1, ..., r_K\})}
\end{equation}

The policy is updated with a clipped objective:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{L}(\theta) = -\mathbb{E}\left[\min\left(\rho_i A_i, \text{clip}(\rho_i, 1-\epsilon, 1+\epsilon) A_i\right)\right] + \beta \cdot D_{KL}(\pi_\theta \| \pi_{\text{ref}})
\end{equation}
where $\rho_i = \frac{\pi_\theta(y_i|x)}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(y_i|x)}$ is the probability ratio.

\subsection{Reward Design}
Our composite reward has three components:

\paragraph{Answer Accuracy ($R_{ans}$):}
\begin{equation}
    R_{ans} = \begin{cases} 1.0 & \text{if defect presence correctly identified} \\ 0.0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Classification Reward ($R_{cls}$):} Type classification accuracy after Hungarian matching between predicted and ground-truth boxes.

\paragraph{Localization Reward ($R_{loc}$):} CIoU-based stepped reward:
\begin{equation}
    R_{loc} = \begin{cases}
        3.0 + 4.0 \cdot CIoU & \text{if } IoU \geq 0.8 \\
        2.0 + 3.0 \cdot CIoU & \text{if } 0.5 \leq IoU < 0.8 \\
        1.0 \cdot CIoU & \text{if } 0.1 \leq IoU < 0.5 \\
        0.0 & \text{if } IoU < 0.1
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Total Reward:} $R = R_{ans} + R_{cls} + R_{loc}$

