% ── 5. Analysis ───────────────────────────────────────
\section{Analysis}

\subsection{RL's Mechanism: Probability Enhancement vs.\ Capability Acquisition}

Our core finding is that RL does not teach the model new detection capabilities.
Instead, it increases the probability of producing high-quality localizations for samples the model already ``almost'' detects correctly.

\textbf{Evidence:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item 88 samples have SFT IoU $\in$ [0.7, 0.8) (borderline)
    \item 49 of these (56\%) are pushed above 0.8 by RL
    \item Only 2 samples improve from IoU=0 (complete miss) to IoU$>$0.9
    \item 127 samples improve, 87 degrade $\rightarrow$ net +40 samples above 0.8
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Analogy:} The SFT model ``can do it but isn't stable.'' RL makes it ``more consistently correct.''

\subsection{Why R@0.5 Barely Changes}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item 70\% of samples already achieve IoU $\geq$ 0.5 under SFT
    \item These samples have advantage $\approx$ 0 in RL (already high reward)
    \item Remaining 30\% with IoU $<$ 0.5 have weak gradient signals
    \item 0.5 is a ``coarse localization'' threshold that SFT already masters
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Degradation Analysis}

24 samples degrade from IoU $\geq$ 0.8 to $<$ 0.8, with 7 dropping below 0.4.
Possible causes include:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Reward hacking:} Model finds shortcuts that maximize reward but reduce IoU
    \item \textbf{Catastrophic forgetting:} Optimizing certain samples damages others
    \item \textbf{Sampling variance:} $K$=4 provides limited advantage estimation
\end{itemize}

% ── 6. Conclusion ─────────────────────────────────────
\section{Conclusion}

We present a GRPO-based RL framework for improving VLM-based industrial anomaly detection.
Our method achieves +5.26pp improvement in R@0.8 through reward-guided training.

Key insights include:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{RL enhances precision, not capability:} The model already has near-correct localization; RL stabilizes it
    \item \textbf{Reward design matters:} Unified IoU-based reward outperforms decoupled sub-dimension rewards
    \item \textbf{Data quality $>$ quantity:} Filtering tiny defects provides marginal but consistent improvement
    \item \textbf{Resolution requires retraining:} Higher resolution inference alone doesn't help without corresponding training
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Future work:}
Two-stage detection (coarse $\rightarrow$ fine) to address resolution limitations,
higher-resolution SFT training to push the capability ceiling,
multi-scale feature fusion in VLM architectures,
and adaptive reward weighting based on defect size.

